  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nEarly afternoon analysis showed a narrow, pesky band of low \nclouds stretching across much of eastern NE into far southwest \nIA with surface ridging/high pressure in control. It will \ncontinue to erode from both the east and west, though thinking \nwe'll start to see low clouds re-develop once the sun sets. The \nareas that are able to clear out may see some fog development \novernight with light southwest winds helping to usher in a tiny \nbit of moisture while temperatures quickly fall. However, there \nare some mid to high level clouds streaming in from the west \nthat could limit fog coverage. So overall confidence in fog \ncoverage is somewhat low, but it does seem favorable to have \nsome in at least parts of the area. In fact, it seems like a \nDense Fog Advisory may be needed in some locations overnight \ninto Saturday morning, just not sure on exact locations yet. \nWith the expected fog and lingering low clouds, forecast lows \nhave ticked up a bit and now remain above just freezing in many \nplaces. That said, it'll be close, so you'll probably want to \ndisconnect any garden hoses you're still using and be alert for\nsome patchy slick spots, especially on bridges and overpasses.\n\nThe surface high will push off to the southeast by Saturday \nmorning with southwesterly flow starting to usher in warmer air.\nCurrently expecting highs in the mid 50s to lower 60s with \nguidance in decent agreement that clouds should start to erode \nthrough the day. However, but given how pesky clouds have been \nthe last couple days, may end up staying a touch cloudier than \npreviously thought, which may keep us a few degrees cooler. \nRegardless, the southwesterly flow should allow us to warm at \nleast a little bit compared to today.\n\nThe warming trend continues Sunday into Monday with highs \ntopping out in the mid 60s to lower 70s on Monday as upper level\nridging edges eastward toward the area. A cold front is then \nprogged to slide though Monday night into Tuesday, though not \nexpecting too much of a dip in temperatures, especially by \nNovember standards, as highs remain in the upper 50s to mid 60s \nthrough the remainder of the week. Despite the frontal passage \nthe forecast remains dry with guidance showing quite a bit of \nlow level dry air remaining in place. A few various ensemble \nmembers suggest a weak shortwave trough could slide through \nsometime Tuesday night into Wednesday, though consensus keeps \nthis wave and associated light rain well to our north. Toward \nthe end of next week, a large scale trough looks to dig into the\neastern CONUS, while it looks like we'll be on the western \nperiphery and remain under northwest flow, guidance does hint at\nsome light spotty precip potentially clipping us, depending how\nfar west the trough extends. Chances remain below 10% though. \nOtherwise, the trough could bring our next chance at a freeze on\nFriday, though the air coming down from Canada doesn't look to \nbe particularly cold at this time.",
